   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Data overview\n",
    "\n",
    "Instructions: REPLACE the contents of this cell with your work, including any updates to recover points lost in your data checkpoint feedback\n"
    "- **Dataset #1**\n",
    "  - **Dataset Name:** California Vehicle Registrations by Fuel Type and ZIP Code\n",
    "  - **Link to the dataset:** https://lab.data.ca.gov/dataset/vehicle-fuel-type-count-by-zip-code\n",
    "  - **Number of observations:** ~4,000,000 rows (raw), 42 rows after cleaning and filtering for San Francisco (6 fuel types × 7 years)\n",
    "  - **Number of variables:** 7 raw (date, zip_code, fuel, vehicles, make, model_year, duty), 4 after cleaning (fuel_type, year, vehicles, percentage)\n",
    "  - **Description of the variables most relevant to this project:** date (standardized registration date used to extract year), zip_code (used to filter for San Francisco ZIP codes starting with 941), fuel (raw fuel type label), and vehicles (count of registered vehicles matching that combination of attributes).\n",
    "  - **Descriptions of any shortcomings this dataset has with respect to the project:** The California DMV does not release actual registration dates and instead standardizes them to a single date per year. The 2018 dataset only records through 1/10/2018, meaning some 2018 registrations appear in the 2019 file. Additionally, the raw dataset contains many granular and inconsistent fuel type labels that required significant normalization before analysis.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Dataset #2**\n",
    "  - **Dataset Name:** Texas Alternatively Fueled Vehicle Registration Dataset\n",
    "  - **Link to the dataset:** https://www.txdmv.gov/sites/default/files/report-files/FY-2023-Alternatively-Fueled-Vehicle-Report.pdf\n",
    "  - **Number of observations:** 144 (18 fuel types × 8 years)\n",
    "  - **Number of variables:** 3 (fuel_type, year, registered_vehicles)\n",
    "  - **Description of the variables most relevant to this project:** fuel_type (the type of fuel used by the vehicle), year (fiscal year 2016–2023), and registered_vehicles (the total number of registered vehicles matching that fuel type for that year).\n",
    "  - **Descriptions of any shortcomings this dataset has with respect to the project:** The TxDMV does not publish county-level or city-level breakdowns by fuel type, meaning Houston-specific data is not publicly available. Texas state-level data is used as a proxy for Houston, which is a limitation given that statewide trends may not perfectly reflect Houston's urban vehicle composition. Additionally, the dataset only covers alternative fuels in detail, with gasoline and other conventional fuels reported as aggregate totals.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Dataset #3**\n",
    "  - **Dataset Name:** U.S. Vehicle Registration Counts by State and Fuel Type (AFDC)\n",
    "  - **Link to the dataset:** https://afdc.energy.gov/vehicle-registration\n",
    "  - **Number of observations:** 468 (52 states/territories × 9 years)\n",
    "  - **Number of variables:** 14 raw (state, year, and one column per fuel type), 4 after cleaning (fuel_type, year, vehicles, percentage)\n",
    "  - **Description of the variables most relevant to this project:** state (filtered to New York as a proxy for New York City), year (2016–2024), and per-fuel-type counts including electric, plug-in hybrid, hybrid electric, gasoline, diesel, flex, and others.\n",
    "  - **Descriptions of any shortcomings this dataset has with respect to the project:** The AFDC dataset is only available at the state level, meaning New York City-specific registration data by fuel type is not available from any public source. New York state is used as a proxy for New York City, which is a limitation since the state includes large rural areas with different vehicle composition than NYC. The NY Open Data Portal DMV dataset was initially considered as a more granular alternative but was found to be unsuitable for year-over-year analysis, as it is a current snapshot of active registrations rather than a historical record, causing severe undercounting in earlier years and artificial inflation in recent years.\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Dataset #4**\n",
    "  - **Dataset Name:** FTA National Transit Database (NTD) Monthly Ridership — Calendar Year UPT\n",
    "  - **Link to the dataset:** https://www.transit.dot.gov/sites/fta.dot.gov/files/2026-02/December%202025%20Complete%20Monthly%20Ridership%20%28with%20adjustments%20and%20estimates%29_260202.xlsx\n",
    "  - **Number of observations:** 24 (1 city × 24 years, 2002–2025)\n",
    "  - **Number of variables:** 3 after cleaning (year, ridership, city)\n",
    "  - **Description of the variables most relevant to this project:** year (calendar year 2002–2025), ridership (total unlinked passenger trips aggregated across all transit modes and service types for the Houston urbanized area), and city (assigned as Houston for schema consistency).\n",
    "  - **Descriptions of any shortcomings this dataset has with respect to the project:** The NTD reports ridership at the urbanized area (UZA) level rather than the city boundary level, meaning the Houston UZA includes surrounding suburbs beyond the city limits. Additionally, ridership figures are aggregated across all modes and service types (bus, demand response, commuter bus, etc.), so mode-level breakdowns are lost after aggregation. NaN values in the raw data — indicating a mode was not yet operational or not reported in a given year — are treated as zero in the sum, which may slightly undercount ridership in early years when reporting coverage was less complete.\n",
    "\n",
    "- Dataset #5\n",
    "  - Dataset Name: Air Quality Statistics by City, 2000-2023\n",
    "  - Link to the dataset: https://www.epa.gov/air-trends/air-quality-cities-and-counties\n",
    "  - Number of observations: 1022\n",
    "  - Number of variables: 27\n",
    "  - Description of the variables most relevant to this project:  Core Based Statistical Area (this shows the city location), Pollutant (NO2, O3, PM 2.5, PM 10), Trend Statistic (Weighted Annual Mean, Annual Mean, 98th Percentile), Year (2000-2023). \n",
    "  - Descriptions of any shortcomings this dataset has with repsect to the project: This dataset measures pollutants with different trend statistics. For instance, NO2 is measured by “Annual Mean”, while PM 2.5 is measured by “Weighted Annual Mean”. The difference in measurement might mean standardizing or further processing of these statistics, or looking at these two pollutants separately. Also, “data from exceptional events are included”, so if there was a wildfire one year for instance in a city, that data is included even though it wouldn’t be an accurate measurement of air quality in general. \n",
    "\n",
    "- Dataset #6\n",
    "  - Dataset Name: Aggregated Adult Smoking Prevalence Dataset\n",
    "  - Link to the dataset: N/A (compiled from Houston State of Health/BRFSS, California Health Interview Survey, and NYC Environment & Health Data Portal)\n",
    "  - Number of observations: 49\n",
    "  - Number of variables: 6\n",
    "  - Description of the variables most relevant to this project: Geography (Harris County TX, San Francisco County CA, New York City NY), Year (2002–2024), Metric (Current Smoker % (Adults)), Source (Original data repository), Current_Smoker_Pct (point estimate ranging from $4.7\\%$ to $21.7\\%$), and Statistically_Unstable (boolean flag indicating statistical reliability).\n",
    "  - Descriptions of any shortcomings this dataset has with respect to the project: Although the metric is now unified (Adult Smoking %), temporal coverage still varies. New York City has 21 points (2002–2022), San Francisco has 18 points (2003–2024), and Harris County has 10 points (2014–2023). While the Houston State of Health data for Harris County is more complete than previous versions, the baseline estimates ($13-18\\%$) are significantly higher than modeled CDC PLACES estimates used in older versions, which should be noted if comparing against older local reports. Additionally, several points in the California data are flagged as statistically unstable due to small sample sizes. \n",
    "\n",
    "- Dataset #7\n",
    "    - Dataset Name: Multi-City Longitudinal Asthma Prevalence Dataset (SF, NY, Houston)\n",
    "    - Link to the dataset: \\\n",
    "         New York: https://a816-dohbesp.nyc.gov/IndicatorPublic/data-explorer/asthma/?id=18#display=summary \\\n",
    "         Houston: https://www.houstontx.gov/planning/Demographics/docs_pdfs/City-Population-Estimates-from-Census-Bureau-2020-to-2022.pdf \\\n",
    "         https://www.houstonstateofhealth.com/indicators/index/view?indicatorId=79&localeId=133891&periodId=246 \\\n",
    "         San Francisco: https://data.chhs.ca.gov/dataset/asthma-prevalence/resource/a440b99b-ccc6-473c-bea1-2baf36b05dbe\n",
    "    - Number of observations: ~40 (approximate total after expanding SF's 2-year intervals: 20 from NYC, 9 from Houston, 11 from SF).\n",
    "    - Number of variables: 4\n",
    "    - Description of the variables most relevant to this project: city (San Francisco, New York City, or Houston), year (the calendar year of data collection, ranging from 2003–2022), age: the demographic filter used for alignment (standardized to adults 18+ years), percentage (percentage of adults reporting a diagnosis and active symptoms or a recent attack).\n",
    "    - Descriptions of any shortcomings this dataset has with respect to the project: The primary shortcoming is the inconsistency in how \"current asthma\" is defined and reported across cities. NYC measures adults who had symptoms in the last 12 months, Houston reports adults told they currently have asthma, and SF uses a 2-year pooled estimate of people who still have asthma or had an attack in the last year. This variation in methodology means \"8% prevalence\" in NYC might not be exactly equivalent to \"8% prevalence\" in Houston. Additionally, the timeframes are mismatched: NYC provides a deep historical look (starting 2003), while Houston (2014) and SF (2015) have much shorter longitudinal windows. Finally, SF's data required manual expansion from 2-year periods to yearly rows, which assumes the prevalence stayed constant across both years.\n",
    "- Dataset #4\n",
    "  - Dataset Name: SF Bart Ridership Data\n",
    "  - Link to the dataset: https://www.bart.gov/about/reports/ridership\n",
    "  - Number of observations: 144 (18 fuel types × 8 years)\n",
    "  - Number of variables: 3 (year, month, avg_weekday_ridership)\n",
    "  - Description of the variables most relevant to this project:** year and month (time) and average weekday ridership (average public transit ridership on weekdays for that month)\n",
    "  - Descriptions of any shortcomings this dataset has with respect to the project: This data captures weekday averages so ignores weekends, and this may underrepresent total system usage and miss behavioral differences between weekday and weekend riders. Ridership changes could reflect service frequency changes, fare changes, or system expansions, which are not controlled for in this dataset."
   ]
