    "### Air Quality Data (#5)\n",
    "\n",
    "This dataset looks at air pollution trends in U.S. cities (called Core Based Statistical Areas, or CBSAs) from 2000 to 2023. Each entry shows a pollutant measured in a specific city along with a statistic that summarizes how that pollutant is evaluated. For instance, one major metric is ozone (O3), which is reported using the “4th Max” value — meaning the fourth-highest daily 8-hour ozone level in a year. This is used instead of the highest reading so that one unusual day doesn't distort the data. The values are around 0.06–0.10 parts per million (ppm). Ozone can irritate the lungs, trigger asthma attacks, and make it harder for people to breathe. Ozone values > 0.07 ppm is considered harmful to breathing, and especially for those with asthma. Ozone values > 0.08 is harmful for everyone, and > 0.3 means reduction in lung function and chest pain. \n",
    "\n",
    "Another metric important to our question is PM 2.5. PM 2.5 values are measured in micrograms per cubic meter (µg/m^3). The “Weighted Annual Mean” represents the average exposure throughout the year. Some cities had averages above 15 µg/m³ in the early 2000s, but many are closer to about 7–10 in recent years. This matters because long-term exposure to PM2.5 is strongly linked to heart disease, lung cancer, and other health problems. 0-12 µg/m^3 is considered satisfactory, 12.1-13.4 µg/m^3 moderate but not good for sensitve people, and > 35 is unhealthy/hazardous. \n",
    "\n",
    "There are a few concerns with this dataset. Firstly, the file notes that the EPA found data quality issues affecting some states, including Florida, Illinois, most of Tennessee, and parts of Kentucky. Measurements from those areas may not be fully reliable. Also, cities have different numbers of monitoring stations, so the data could be not consistent between cities. Another limitation is that the dataset relies on summary statistics rather than raw daily readings. While this makes it easier to see long-term trends, it can hide short-term spikes such as wildfire smoke events."
   ]
  },
