   "source": [
    "### Aggregated Smoking & Secondhand Smoke Exposure Dataset (#6)\n",
    "\n",
    "This dataset was compiled from three separate public health survey sources to track adult cigarette smoking prevalence across Harris County (TX), San Francisco County (CA), and New York City (NY). It covers years ranging from 2002 to 2024. The primary metric is current smoker prevalence (%), representing the percentage of adults who reported being current smokers.\n",
    "* New York City: Data is from the NYC Environment & Health Data Portal, with a robust series of 21 data points from 2002 to 2022 (range: $8.8\\%$ to $21.7\\%$).\n",
    "* San Francisco: Data is from the California Health Interview Survey (CHIS), with 18 data points from 2003 to 2024 (range: $4.7\\%$ to $13.5\\%$).\n",
    "* Harris County: Data is from the Houston State of Health portal (BRFSS), providing a continuous 10-year series from 2014 to 2023 (range: $13.6\\%$ to $18.1\\%$).\n",
    "#### Improvements: \n",
    "This update significantly improves the dataset's utility. First, all three geographies now use the same metric (Smoking Prevalence), whereas NYC previously used secondhand smoke exposure. Second, the Harris County series no longer has a gap for 2018–2019, providing a much smoother trend line for analysis.\n",
    "#### Limitations: \n",
    "The total observation count is 49. While the metrics are aligned, researchers should still account for slight differences in survey methodology between the three states. Approximately $8\\%$ of the observations (primarily in the San Francisco data) are flagged as statistically unstable, meaning the sample sizes were small and these specific values should be interpreted with caution."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 13,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "\"\"\"\n",
    "Combines three smoking-related datasets into a single long-format CSV.\n",
    "\n",
    "Sources:\n",
    "  1. houston_smoking.csv            – Houston County, TX current smoker %\n",
    "  2. AskCHISResults*.csv            – San Francisco County CHIS current smoker %\n",
    "  3. NYC_EH_Data_Portal_*.csv       – NYC, NY current smoker %\n",
    "\n",
    "Output: combined_smoking_data.csv\n",
    "\"\"\"\n",
    "\n",
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "import re\n",
    "\n",
    "# ── 1. Houston, TX ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n",
    "# Load the updated Houston dataset (2014-2023)\n",
    "houston = pd.read_csv('data/00-raw/' + 'houston_smoking.csv')\n",
    "\n",
    "# Ensure the columns match the required structure\n",
    "houston.columns = ['Year', 'Current_Smoker_Pct']\n",
    "houston['Year'] = houston['Year'].astype(int)\n",
    "houston['Geography'] = 'Houston, TX'\n",
    "houston['Source'] = 'Houston State of Health / BRFSS'\n",
    "houston['Metric'] = 'Current Smoker (%)'\n",
    "\n",
    "# All estimates in this specific series are considered statistically stable\n",
    "houston['Statistically_Unstable'] = False\n",
    "\n",
    "# Reorder columns to match the aggregated format\n",
    "houston = houston[['Year', 'Geography', 'Source', 'Metric', 'Current_Smoker_Pct', 'Statistically_Unstable']]"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The date column contains full registration dates (MM/DD/YYYY). Since our analysis focuses on year-over-year trends, we extract just the year."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 15,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# ── 2. San Francisco County ───────────────────────────────────────────\n",
    "# parse excel into csv style\n",
    "san_fran_raw = pd.read_excel('data/00-raw/' + 'AskCHISResults202602191325.csv', header=None)\n",
    "san_fran_data = san_fran_raw.iloc[7:25, [0, 1]].copy()\n",
    "san_fran_data.columns = ['Label', 'Pct']\n",
    "san_fran_data = san_fran_data[san_fran_data['Label'].str.contains('Current Smoker', na=False)]\n",
    "\n",
    "def parse_san_fran(label, pct):\n",
    "    year = int(re.search(r'\\((\\d{4})\\)', str(label)).group(1))\n",
    "    unstable = '*' in str(pct)\n",
    "    pct_val = float(re.sub(r'[^\\d.]', '', str(pct)))\n",
    "    return year, pct_val, unstable\n",
    "\n",
    "rows = [parse_san_fran(r['Label'], r['Pct']) for _, r in san_fran_data.iterrows()]\n",
