  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Based on the generated visualizations, we can draw the following conclusions regarding the dataset and the public health landscape:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. General Downward Trajectory\n",
    "All three cities exhibit a clear long-term decline in adult smoking. New York City provides the most robust historical context, showing a steady decrease from the early 2000s. The visualization confirms that urban tobacco control measures (taxes, smoking bans, etc.) have been broadly effective across different regions.\n",
    "\n",
    "2. Geographic Disparities & Baselines\n",
    "Houston (Harris County): Despite having the shortest data series (starting in 2014), Houston maintains the highest prevalence among the three. The baseline of 13–18% is significantly higher than its coastal counterparts, suggesting different socio-economic factors or less restrictive local tobacco policies.\n",
    "\n",
    "San Francisco & NYC: Both coastal cities show lower overall prevalence. However, San Francisco’s trend line may appear \"choppy\" or have gaps due to the removal of statistically unstable data points.\n",
    "\n",
    "3. Impact of Data Limitations\n",
    "The \"Unstable\" Gap: By filtering out Statistically_Unstable rows, we improved the accuracy of the estimates but sacrificed temporal density in the San Francisco subset. This highlights a trade-off: we have a cleaner \"truth,\" but less ability to analyze year-over-year volatility in CA.\n",
    "\n",
    "The Houston Baseline Shift: As noted in the metadata, the Houston values are higher than previous CDC modeled estimates. The visualization makes this gap starkly visible, alerting researchers that comparing this dataset to older reports requires a disclaimer regarding the change in methodology (BRFSS vs. PLACES).\n",
    "\n",
    "4. Convergence vs. Divergence\n",
    "The boxplot reveals that NYC has the widest historical variance (due to its long 21-year tracking), while Houston’s data is clustered at a higher range. There is no evidence of \"convergence\" yet; the gap between Houston and San Francisco remains roughly consistent over the last decade."
   ]
